    "title": "Carnival Cruise Line announces first sensory-friendly destination",
    "text": "Related video: Carnival Cruise Line makes changes to Tampa sailings, offering longer cruises MIAMI, Fla. (WFLA) — In honor of World Autism Acceptance Month, Carnival Cruise Line announced on Thursday that one of its destinations is officially certified as the first to earn KultureCity’s prestigious sensory inclusion certification. Celebration Key, the cruise line’s exclusive destination in Grand Bahama, was awarded this groundbreaking achievement for its commitment to making the experience inclusive and fun for every guest including families with sensory needs. KultureCity is a leading nonprofit organization that has received worldwide recognition for certifying venues that create sensory-inclusive spaces for those with disabilities such as autism, PTSD, dementia, and other sensory processing disorders. Credit: … Carnival Cruise Line has been in partnership with the organization for the past six years. At Celebration Key, more than 400 team members have been trained to help guests with sensory needs. Complimentary guest sensory bags are also available on location, packed with noise-reducing headphones, fidget tools, and glasses with filters to block out bright lights. A VIP lanyard featuring a headphone logo and light bulb symbol are also included in the bags to discreetly signal to Carnival employees that the guest may require additional support or accommodations. “Creating truly inclusive experiences means anticipating the needs of every guest before they arrive — and meeting them with empathy, understanding, and thoughtful support,” said Uma Srivastava, executive director of KultureCity. “By certifying Celebration Key, Carnival Cruise Line is setting a new standard for the travel industry, ensuring that individuals with sensory needs and invisible disabilities can fully participate in and enjoy every moment of their journey.”",
